// stemcase.nv — the case contract, published, because the reference
// implementations leave it unwritten and a caller who gets it wrong
// is not told.
//
// `stem` DOES NOT LOWER-CASE, AND SAYS SO OUT LOUD
//
// Every Snowball algorithm is defined on lower-case input.  The
// upstream generated stemmers do not lower-case; `rust-stemmers` does
// not; `snowballstemmer` does not.  In all three, `Running` goes in
// and `Running` comes out, because no suffix in the step tables is
// spelt with a capital.  Nothing is raised, nothing is logged, and
// the caller has just written a second, unrelated term into an index
// that already contains `run`.
//
// This package could lower-case inside `stem` and make the problem
// vanish.  It does not, for two reasons and the second is the
// decisive one.
//
// First, a stem is a token and the caller usually holds the case for
// its own purposes — a highlighter wants the original, an indexer
// wants both.  Folding inside `stem` would mean the function no
// longer answers a function OF ITS ARGUMENT in the caller's alphabet.
//
// Second, and this is the one: lower-casing correctly is
// LANGUAGE-DEPENDENT and this package takes no Unicode dependency.
// Turkish lowers `I` to `ı` and `İ` to `i`; every other language's
// rule gets both of those wrong.  A `stem` that folded would have to
// either pick a rule and be silently wrong for Turkish, or take the
// 245 KB of tables that novo.toml's dependency comment refuses.
//
// So `stemword.stem` REFUSES a word with an upper-case letter in it,
// with `StemNeedsLowercase`, and this module is what a caller uses to
// satisfy it.  A refusal is a loud wrong answer, and a loud wrong
// answer is the only kind anybody fixes.

use stemlang

// The word with every ASCII letter `A`..`Z` mapped to `a`..`z`, and
// no other byte changed.
//
// This is the whole fold for English, Porter and every Latin-alphabet
// language on the list, because the Snowball algorithms for those
// languages either use no letters outside ASCII or treat the accented
// ones as distinct letters that are already lower-case.
//
// It is NOT the fold for Turkish, whose `I` must become `ı` and not
// `i`, and it does nothing at all for Greek, Russian, Armenian,
// Arabic, Hindi, Nepali, Tamil or Yiddish.  `needs_unicode_fold`
// names exactly that set.
//
// ```novo
// use stemcase
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println(stemcase.fold_ascii("Running"))      // running
//     println(stemcase.fold_ascii("ANDES"))        // andes
// ```
pub fn fold_ascii(word: Str) -> Str []
    todo("stemmer-nv.stemcase.fold_ascii")

// Whether the word is in the form `stemword.stem` accepts for this
// language.
//
// False when the word is empty, when it holds an ASCII space or tab,
// and when it holds an ASCII letter `A`..`Z`.  Those are exactly the
// three token refusals in `stemerror.StemError`, so a caller that
// filters on this predicate never meets one.
//
// For a language where `needs_unicode_fold` is true, being prepared
// is necessary and not sufficient: this package cannot see that `Ω`
// is upper-case, so a Greek word that has not been folded passes here
// and stems wrongly.  `fold_note` is the sentence that says what to
// do about that.
//
// ```novo
// use stemcase
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${stemcase.is_prepared(StemEnglish, "running")}")        // true
//     println("${stemcase.is_prepared(StemEnglish, "Running")}")        // false
//     println("${stemcase.is_prepared(StemEnglish, "running fast")}")   // false
// ```
pub fn is_prepared(lang: stemlang.StemLang, word: Str) -> Bool []
    todo("stemmer-nv.stemcase.is_prepared")

// Whether preparing a word in this language needs more than
// `fold_ascii`.
//
// True for Greek, Turkish, Russian, Armenian, Arabic, Hindi, Nepali,
// Tamil and Yiddish — every language whose alphabet is not Latin,
// plus Turkish, whose alphabet is Latin and whose case mapping is
// not the common one.  False for English, Porter and the other Latin
// languages.
//
// A caller writing a multi-language indexer branches on this once,
// per language, at start-up: false means `fold_ascii` is enough and
// the program carries no Unicode tables; true means it reaches for
// unicode-nv.
//
// ```novo
// use stemcase
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${stemcase.needs_unicode_fold(StemEnglish)}")   // false
//     println("${stemcase.needs_unicode_fold(StemTurkish)}")   // true
// ```
pub fn needs_unicode_fold(lang: stemlang.StemLang) -> Bool []
    todo("stemmer-nv.stemcase.needs_unicode_fold")

// One sentence saying what a caller must do to a word in this
// language before stemming it, ending without a full stop.
//
// For English: that ASCII lower-casing is the whole preparation.  For
// Turkish: that the dotted and dotless `i` make a locale-free fold
// wrong.  For Greek: that the algorithm removes diacritics itself but
// expects lower-case input.  The text is written for a log line or a
// comment in a caller's configuration, and it is the documentation of
// the refused dependency at the place a reader meets the problem.
//
// ```novo
// use stemcase
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println(stemcase.fold_note(StemTurkish))
// ```
pub fn fold_note(lang: stemlang.StemLang) -> Str []
    todo("stemmer-nv.stemcase.fold_note")
